.THS Sys 5L News.SH NAMESys - this file describes the mechanism to forward received articles toother sites or to programs..SH DESCRIPTION.ti +3This files specifies the newsgroups and distributions to be forwarded toanother site or to be pipe'd to a program. So you are able to limit theamount of articles to be forwarded by area (distribution) or/and thetopics (newsgroups)..SH FORMATThe format of the file is.ce<system>{> <flags>}:<newsgroups>:<dists>{:<program>}where.B <system>is in general the system to forward the news (or an ID for programs).You can append a list of other systemnames (seperated by a slash.B /form the main system), which are seperated by commas to stop forwardingarticles which had already passed these systems. The.B <flags>are a list of flags which are inserted before the filename in thebatchfile. These flags are interpreted by sbatch only. The.B <newsgroups>is a list of newsgroups which are in regular expression format (usingHenry Spencer's regular expression library), seperated by spaces.Internal the expression will be enclosed with.B ^and.bold\$to allow only exact matches..B <dists>are the distributions which aren't regular expressions, but they maycontain the meta characters.B ?and.B *to match one (?) or zero or more (*) characters. The <newsgroups> and<dists> field allow a not operation by prefixing an expression with an.B !so you can select specific items not to be forwarded. If an articleshould be forwarded the filename is written in a file in.B /h0/SPOOL/BATCHwith the filename equal to <system> (if possible). This file is used by.B sbatchto send the articles themselves..PPThe.B <program>is optional but if it is given the filename of the article isn't writtento the file mentioned above but the article itself is pipe'd to thisprogram (so the program must read the article from stdin)..PPIf the first character of <program> is a plus sign then the rest will beused as a full path to a directory where to store the articles withincreasing numbers. If the first character is a minus sign then the restis a full path to a directory which is used instead of the batchdirectory (/h0/SPOOL/BATCH)..PPEach line in this file may be continued in the next line if the lastcharacter in the prvious line had been a backslash..PPIf a line starts with a colon.RB ( : )then the rest of the line is taken as a filename of a file to beincluded.  With this features it is possible to create one file for eachsystem which can be changed from remote via mail.  For this purpose theprogram.B remsyscan be found in this distribution..SH EXAMPLES.raw 4myfeed:!local.* to\\.myfeed !to\\..* .*:!local *subfeed:(sub|muc)\\..*:sub muc worldsave:.*source.*:*:save_it.noraw.B myfeedwill get everything expect these newsgroups which are prefixed by.B localor have distribution.B localor have the prefix.B to.and not followed by myfeed..B subfeedwill only receive groups of a local are (Newsgroups prefixed by muc. orsub. and distribution muc sub and world). The entry.B savewill store (assuming a proper program) all articles in source groups.